course: dictionary
lesson: 10909
title: Visually: with your eyes
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Visually comes from visual, which has to do with seeing. The -ly ending turns it into an adverb, so it tells you how something is seen or checked. Look at the words around it to find its meaning.
explain: «Check the bike visually before you ride.» Here visually means by looking with your eyes. You are not checking it by touch or by sound. «The poster is visually striking» means the poster looks striking to the eye, so it catches your attention.
explain: «Visually impaired» means partly or fully blind. It is the official term, and it describes a person without a rude label. Some soft words, such as «hard of hearing», are respectful in the same way.
fact: Visually means in a way that has to do with seeing or how something looks.
fact: «Visually» can also mean by looking with your eyes, as in «check the bike visually».
fact: «Visually striking» means it catches the eye.
fact: «Visually impaired» means partly or fully blind, and it is the official, respectful term.
fact: In maths, information shown visually is in a chart, graph or picture rather than in words.
exercise: «Check the bike visually before you ride.» What does visually mean here? | By counting the wheels | Very quickly, in a rush | *By looking with your eyes | By listening carefully
exercise: «The poster is visually striking.» Which is closest in meaning? | It smells nice | It is very loud | *It looks striking to the eye | It is written in French
exercise: Why is «visually impaired» a respectful term? | It means the person is very clever | It is a word for a fast car | It means the person cannot speak | *It describes a person without a rude label
test: The results are shown visually on a graph. What does this mean? | Read aloud by a teacher | Written in very long sentences | Hidden in a box | *Shown in a picture or chart, not in words
test: What does «visually impaired» mean? | *Partly or fully blind | Good at drawing | Speaking a different language | Very tired
exam: Which phrase describes a person who is partly or fully blind, in a respectful way? | loudly hungry | *visually impaired | visually tired | very noisy
